{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC(a) measurement-only instrumentation pushed: Gerrit change 2589 — https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/2589 (commit b4fed2147007, 6 files +732/-11). Per the operator's decision: MEASURE FIRST, choose limits later. No mem_limit, no instance-size change, no fan-out change.\n\n## What now publishes, on the existing 5-minute timer, namespace rebar/host\n\n    mem_available_percent          (no dims, Percent)  from `free -k` column 7\n    mem_used_percent               (no dims, Percent)  complement\n    mem_probe_ok                   (no dims, 1/0)      did the read actually succeed\n    container_memory_rss_bytes     (dim: container)    one datapoint per running container\n    container_stats_ok             (no dims, 1/0)      the census's own heartbeat\n\nHost gauges are DIMENSIONLESS, matching root_disk_used_percent and every rebar/host alarm in monitoring*.tf — a dimension on one side only silently unmatches. The per-container gauge follows the other local precedent, disk_used_percent's `mount` dimension, because 'which resident set grew' is a comparison ACROSS containers: one `Sum by container` query, versus per-service metric names that would need this probe and every consumer edited whenever compose gains or renames a service.\n\n## Three details that make the measurement trustworthy\n\n1. **It reads `available`, not `free`.** Column 7 of `free -k`, not column 4. On Linux those differ by the reclaimable page cache, and using `free` would have systematically understated headroom — the RED proof for this shows [6] vs [25], so it is not a rounding difference.\n2. **`mem_probe_ok` distinguishes a measured zero from a synthesised one.** Both host gauges publish on every tick including failure paths, in each gauge's pessimistic direction (0% available / 100% used) — the same convention section 5's mirror_out_of_sync now uses. But a pessimistic default must never be mistaken for a real reading, so any analysis of this campaign's data must DROP ticks where mem_probe_ok = 0. A genuinely full box publishes a real 0 with mem_probe_ok = 1.\n3. **peak_delta_bytes is reported alongside the absolute peak.** `RUSAGE_SELF.ru_maxrss` is a process-wide HIGH-WATER MARK, so in a long-lived MCP server the absolute figure attributes every prior gate run's cost to whichever run you happen to be reading. Without the delta the numbers would be actively misleading for exactly the question being asked.\n\n## Peak RSS per gate run\n\nNew `src/rebar/llm/peak_rss.py` emits a `GATE_PEAK_RSS {json}` journald marker at gate completion — gate, ticket_id, peak_rss_bytes, peak_delta_bytes, elapsed_ms, timestamp. A marker rather than metric plumbing because the containers deliberately hold no AWS credentials (IMDS hop limit), which is why VOTER_ERROR and MERGE_CHANGE_ERROR are markers the host probe greps and republishes; the convention is copied verbatim from voter_merge.merge_change_error, including the f829 rule that the logger copy omits the token. It also works under the CLI on a laptop where CloudWatch does not exist.\n\nSeam: the LIBRARY entry points `rebar.llm.review_plan` and `rebar.llm.verify_completion` are wrapped rather than _spawn_gate_daemon and the CLI separately, since both call paths reach the gate through those two functions. Emitted from a `finally`, so runs that DIE — the OOM case, the whole reason this exists — still report.\n\n`ru_maxrss_to_bytes(value, platform)` is a pure function: bytes on darwin/BSD, x1024 on Linux. Kept pure so both branches are testable without the platform.\n\n## docker stats is bounded twice\n\n`--no-stream` plus `timeout 15`, matching the bare `timeout N docker ...` idiom autodeploy.sh already uses in four places. If `timeout` were itself absent the command fails 127, container_stats_ok publishes 0, and the probe continues — it can never wedge the timer. A test asserts both bounds by hanging the stub for 300s unless invoked through timeout AND with --no-stream.\n\n## Eight RED proofs, all binding\n\npublish-nothing-on-failure -> 4 failed ([] != [0]); read `free` not `available` -> 2 failed ([6] != [25]); drop --no-stream -> TimeoutExpired after 60s, the wedge reproduced; gate container_stats_ok on success -> 4 failed; treat ru_maxrss as bytes everywhere -> 3 failed (2048 != 2097152); emit on `else` not `finally` -> 1 failed; logger copy carries the token -> 1 failed (f829 regression); drop the wrapper from review_plan -> 1 failed. All restored, 24/24 green.\n\n## Verification\n\npytest tests/scripts 1369 passed / 3 skipped; test_gate_peak_rss_9ea3.py 12 passed; make lint exit 0 (mechanism new=0 so NO marker needed, complexity new=0/increased=0); make typecheck 750 files. One caveat carried forward honestly: a full `pytest tests/unit` sweep was started as an extra check and its result was not seen — CI will settle it.\n\n## Next step is DATA, not a decision\n\nOnce this lands and deploys, the campaign needs to run long enough to answer: peak MCP RSS during a gate, steady-state RSS per container, and how RSS scales with ticket size. Only then can a mem_limit or an instance size be chosen as anything other than a guess."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788463506316326001,"uuid":"44857833-f428-44fc-9618-8a83a2190794"}